course: everyday
lesson: 448
title: The magician's trick explained
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: At a birthday party in Leeds, a magician pulled a coin out of Owen's ear. Owen checked his ear for the rest of the day. Afterwards the magician showed him: the coin had been in his hand the whole time, tucked between two fingers, while everyone watched his other hand waving. «So it's not magic, it's practice!» said Owen, delighted. A magician is an honest trickster: everybody knows it's a trick, everybody agreed to be fooled, and everything is given back. That is what makes it fun and not a con.
explain: The heart of most tricks is misdirection — making you look one way while the secret happens somewhere else. Ruby's class watched a famous magician on TV who explains his own tricks. «I'm a professional liar,» he said, «but I tell you I'm lying.» He showed how a big movement with one hand pulls every eye, while the other hand does the quiet work. Mia «picked any card» at a party and the magician «guessed» it; her uncle explained that the magician had steered her to that card without her noticing. Mia then spotted the same idea in shops, which put the things they want you to choose right at eye level.
explain: Some «games» use the same skills to take money. In a big city, Jake's family watched a man with three cups and a ball. People were betting and «winning». Jake's dad said those winners were the man's friends, pretending, so others would join in — and a tourist lost a lot of money while they watched. «Never bet on street games.» A man in the park offered Isla a «mind-reading» game for a pound: he guessed her number wrong twice, then said «Third time pays double!» Isla said «Nah, I'm good» and kept her pound. «One more go» and «double or nothing» are how such games win.
explain: Fairs and screens fool people too. At a fair in Texas, the hoop-toss hoops were just barely too small for the bottle necks; Kai's dad said «They're set up so most folks miss.» Next time, Kai watched for five minutes first — nobody won. A man guessing weights «within 2 pounds or you win a prize» guessed Ella's dad wrong; the prize was a tiny plastic toy. «I won! A buck's worth of plastic for five bucks,» Dad laughed. Leo watched a video of a man floating in the air twenty times; his sister said «It's camera angles and a hidden stand, mate.» A fortune teller told Aisha's aunt «You've had a difficult time recently, but someone close is thinking of you.» Aisha's mum said «That's true of everyone, love.»
explain: Some tricks are gifts. When Noah was three, his grandad did the «disappearing thumb» trick, and Noah screamed with laughter. At six he saw the thumb was just folded — and now he does it for his baby cousin. A magician in Dublin made a girl's watch vanish from a box and appear in his pocket; she was worried for one second, and he gave it straight back with a bow. A real magician gives it back. A con artist — someone who tricks people to take their money — doesn't.
fact: Magic tricks are deception people agree to enjoy: everyone knows it's a trick, and everything is given back.
fact: A con is a trick that takes something from people who did not agree to be fooled.
fact: Misdirection means making people look in one place while the secret move happens in another.
fact: A magician can steer a «free» choice, such as which card you pick, without you noticing.
fact: In street betting games with cups and a ball, the «winners» are often the trickster's helpers, and ordinary players lose.
fact: «One more go» and «double or nothing» are ways to keep people paying.
fact: Fairground games are set up so the stall makes money; most people lose, and prizes are often worth less than a go.
fact: Watching a game before paying shows whether anyone ever wins.
fact: Videos can be edited and filmed from one angle, so seeing something on a screen does not prove it is real.
fact: Fortune tellers often say things that fit almost anyone, and listeners fill in the details themselves.
fact: Knowing how tricks work helps you spot the same tricks in adverts, shops and scams.
fact: Some tricks, like a grandad's «disappearing thumb», are loving games for small children.
fact: «Folks» (US) means people; «a buck» (US) is a dollar; «nah, I'm good» means no, thank you.
exercise: Where was the coin really before it «came out of» Owen's ear? | In the magician's hand, between two fingers | Inside Owen's ear the whole time | Up the magician's sleeve in a little tube on a string | Under Owen's party hat
exercise: Why is a magic show not a con? | Everyone agrees to be fooled and gets everything back | Magicians never take any money | Magic is real, so it isn't a trick and nobody is fooled | The audience can't see what happens
exercise: What is misdirection? | Making you look one way while the secret happens elsewhere | Giving someone directions to the wrong place so they get lost on purpose | Doing a trick very slowly so it's clear | Telling the audience the secret first
exercise: What did the famous magician on TV say about himself? | I'm a professional liar, but I tell you I'm lying | I have real magic powers from my grandmother, but I keep them secret | I never use any tricks at all | I can read every person's mind
exercise: How did the magician «guess» Mia's card? | He steered her to pick it without her noticing | He read her mind with a special power | She told him the card by accident while she was laughing | Every card in the pack was the same
exercise: Where did Mia notice the same «steering» idea afterwards? | In shops, with things at eye level | In her maths homework at school | At the swimming pool, in the changing room | In the weather forecast
exercise: Who were the «winners» in the three-cups game? | The man's friends, pretending | Lucky tourists from abroad | Children who watched closely | Police officers in disguise
exercise: What did Jake's dad say about street betting games? | Never bet on them | Bet only a little at first | Watch three times, then bet | Bet only if you see the ball
exercise: What was the trick in Isla's «mind-reading» game? | «Third time pays double» kept her paying | He really could read minds | The numbers were written down first, so it was a fair game | The game was free for children
exercise: Why couldn't Kai win the hoop toss? | The hoops were just too small for the bottles | Kai threw from too close | The bottles were glued down to the shelf with strong glue | It was too windy that day
exercise: What clever thing did Kai do at the next fair? | Watched first to see if anyone won | Brought his own hoops | Paid for ten goes at once, for a better price | Stood closer to the bottles
exercise: Why did Ella's dad laugh about «winning»? | The prize was worth less than the go | He won a huge teddy bear | He guessed his own weight and won a goldfish | The man gave his money back
exercise: How was the «floating man» video made? | Camera angles and a hidden stand | A real man who can float | A very strong wind | Balloons inside his coat
exercise: Why did Aisha's aunt gasp at the fortune teller? | The words seemed to fit her, but they fit everyone | The fortune teller knew her phone number | The fortune teller had met her before, at a friend's party last year | The crystal ball showed her picture
exercise: What was the secret of grandad's «disappearing thumb»? | The thumb was just folded | The thumb went in his pocket | It was a plastic thumb on top | Noah closed his eyes at the wrong time
exercise: What is the difference between the Dublin magician and a con artist? | The magician gave the watch straight back | The magician kept the watch for a day, then sold it in a shop | The con artist always wears a cape | There is no difference at all
test: Owen, 6: «he really pulled money outta my ear, I'm gonna be rich». What's true? | It was a trick — the coin was in his hand | Ears do make coins sometimes | Only magicians' ears make coins, because they practise | Owen should check every day
test: Man on the street: «Find the ball, double your money! Look, that fella just won!» What's going on? | The «winner» is likely his helper — walk on | It's a fair game if you watch closely | You win if you pick the middle cup | It's safe because someone won
test: Fair stall: «Three hoops, a dollar! Easy win, folks!» What's the smart thing to do first? | Watch for a while to see if anyone wins | Pay for three goes at once | Throw as hard as you can | Ask the man to promise you'll win at least once
test: Fortune teller: «Someone close is thinking of you.» Aisha's mum: «That's true of everyone.» What does she mean? | The words fit almost anybody | The fortune teller can see the future | Everyone has the very same best friend | She's secretly a fortune teller too
test: Leo: «I saw him FLOAT, with my own eyes, on YouTube». What's the problem with this? | A video can be edited or filmed from one angle | YouTube never has any tricks | If you see it with your own eyes, it must be true | Only TV can show tricks
test: Park man: «Wrong again? Double or nothing, love!» What's he trying to do? | Keep you paying so he wins | Help you win your money back | Give you a free extra go at the end | Teach you a clever maths trick
test: What would happen if a magician kept the girl's watch and walked off? | It would be stealing, not a trick | It would be part of a normal show | It would be a better magic trick | It would be a gift for the magician
test: Magician: «Pick ANY card you like!» Which is true? | Your choice may have been steered | Your choice is always totally free | Magicians never touch the cards | All the cards have the same picture
test: In a cartoon, a wizard has real magic and makes a car vanish. What's the truth about stage magic? | It's practice and misdirection, not powers | Some magicians have real powers | Stage magic is done by robots | Magicians use special glasses
test: Noah's baby cousin squeals at the «disappearing thumb». Is Noah being a liar? | No — it's a loving game everyone enjoys | Yes, and he should stop at once | Yes, he's conning his cousin | No, because the thumb really goes
test: TV magician: «Watch my right hand!» Where should a clever kid look? | At the other hand | At the right hand only | At the magician's hat | At the people in the audience
exam: Identify the principal distinction between a theatrical magic performance and a confidence trick. | The audience consents to be deceived and nothing is retained by the performer | The magician invariably performs without remuneration, whereas the confidence trickster is paid | The confidence trickster invariably works alone | No distinction can be drawn between them
exam: It is given that, in a street betting game, several bystanders are observed to win. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The apparent winners may be accomplices, and ordinary participants generally lose | The game is evidently fair to all participants | The probability of winning is one in three | Participation is advisable after observation
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the technique termed «misdirection»? | The diversion of attention from the place where the concealed action occurs | The provision of incorrect directions to a location, so that the audience is led astray | The performance of a trick at reduced speed | The disclosure of the method prior to performance
exam: Determine which of the following does NOT constitute a sound inference concerning a video depicting a person floating unsupported. | That the video proves the person can float | That the video may have been edited | That a support may be concealed from the camera | That a single camera angle may conceal the method
